Preterm Labour
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Regular painful contractions <37 weeks, Progressive cervical dilation, Positive fetal fibronectin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-043
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of regular, painful uterine contractions and pelvic pressure at 33 weeks gestation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and husband
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated regular, painful abdominal cramping occurring every 5 minutes in a 33-week pregnant patient, accompanied by a new, heavy pelvic pressure. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively identify active preterm labor, rule out simple Braxton Hicks contractions, and immediately coordinate ambulance transport to a tertiary maternity unit for tocolysis and fetal neuroprotection before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am only 33 weeks, but my stomach has been tightening painfully every five minutes for the last two hours, and I feel like the baby is pushing down.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the onset of regular painful uterine contractions beginning approximately two hours ago. Currently at 33 weeks gestation, she notes these contractions are increasing in frequency and intensity, now occurring exactly 5 minutes apart. She describes a continuous, low, dull backache radiating to the abdomen. She complains of intense pelvic pressure and a sensation of fetal descent. She reports a noticeable increase in vaginal discharge, which has become thick and mucoid. She denies any sudden gush of fluid (membranes appear intact) or bright red bleeding.
Functional Status: Pacing the room, stopping to breathe through the painful contractions.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of preterm delivery requiring neonatal intensive care; husband is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, visibly pausing and grimacing during a contraction that lasts for 45 seconds.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals palpable hardening of the uterus during a contraction. The uterus is soft and non-tender between contractions. Fetal heart rate via handheld Doppler is 140 bpm, regular, with accelerations noted. Speculum examination (performed carefully) reveals thick mucoid discharge at the os; no pooling of amniotic fluid. Visual inspection suggests the cervix is effacing, though digital examination for dilation is deferred to the receiving hospital to avoid stimulating further labor or introducing infection.
Environmental Observations: Hospital bags are not packed; clear distress in the household.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prenatal vitamins.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Active Preterm Labor (33 weeks gestation) - Status: Active, contracting regularly
Differentials:

Braxton Hicks Contractions - Ruled out by the painful, regular, and progressively intensifying nature of the contractions, accompanied by the distinct pelvic pressure and backache.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out as the primary cause of contractions by the absence of dysuria and the sheer regularity and intensity of the uterine activity, though a UTI can trigger labor.

Placental Abruption - Ruled out by the complete relaxation of the uterus between contractions and the absence of continuous, rigid pain or dark bleeding.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Requires urgent neonatal capability)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is exhibiting the classic diagnostic tetrad of active preterm labor: regular, painful contractions prior to 37 weeks, accompanied by pelvic pressure and mucoid discharge (loss of mucus plug). The objective clinical observation of palpable, regular uterine hardening lasting 45 seconds confirms true labor activity. Urgent after-hours presence was mandatory to confirm the diagnosis, reassure the patient regarding the fetal heart rate, and immediately activate priority EMS transport to a tertiary hospital equipped with a Level 3 NICU. This patient requires immediate in-hospital interventions (tocolytics to stop contractions, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity, and magnesium sulfate for fetal neuroprotection) that absolutely cannot be delayed.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for priority transport to the designated tertiary maternity hospital. Placed the patient in a left lateral resting position to optimize placental blood flow. Deferred digital cervical examination to the receiving obstetric team. Provided comprehensive clinical handover to the paramedics upon their arrival.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the pressure on your cervix, you must stop pacing and lie down on your left side immediately; experiencing the reduction in gravity pulling the baby down will reinforce the need for strict bed rest while we wait. To help the hospital team act quickly, do not eat or drink anything right now; understanding that you may need urgent medications or procedures will ensure your stomach remains empty.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on scene.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew.

